From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 08:35:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] gsupplicant: keep the connected network on a security change

signal_bss_changed() removes a network whenever the security computed
for one of its BSSs changes, and adds the BSS back as a new network.
For the BSS we are associated with that takes the connected service
down with it: connman disconnects, drops the address and offers the
same access point again under the other security.

The associated BSS cannot really change its security while we stay
associated. An access point that is reconfigured drops its stations
first, so CurrentBSS goes to "/" and current_network is cleared before
the new security shows up. A change reported while still connected
comes from a bad scan result.

Seen with MediaTek's wlan_drv_gen2 (MT6739): after an active scan
while connected it reports the associated BSS with its IEs cut short,
the SSID element followed by zero padding, while every other BSS of
the same scan is complete. wpa_supplicant then publishes an empty RSN
KeyMgmt with Privacy still set, bss_compute_security() returns WEP and
the WPA2 network is removed. Any scan while connected (a settings
page refreshing its list is enough) drops Wi-Fi.

Ignore the change for the associated BSS of the current network and
log it. The security is computed again on every property change, so
the next good scan result brings the BSS back in line, and a genuine
change is applied by the first update after the association has gone.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 gsupplicant/supplicant.c | 23 ++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/gsupplicant/supplicant.c b/gsupplicant/supplicant.c
index 9d9c99e..2ea231c 100644
--- a/gsupplicant/supplicant.c
+++ b/gsupplicant/supplicant.c
@@ -3076,7 +3076,28 @@ static void signal_bss_changed(const char *path, DBusMessageIter *iter)
 	old_security = network->security;
 	bss_compute_security(bss);
 
-	if (old_security != bss->security) {
+	/*
+	 * The BSS we are associated with cannot change its security
+	 * without the association going down first: an access point
+	 * that is reconfigured drops its stations, CurrentBSS goes to
+	 * "/" and current_network is cleared. A change reported while
+	 * still connected comes from a bad scan result, for example a
+	 * driver that reports the connected BSS with its IEs cut short
+	 * after an active scan, so the RSN element is missing and only
+	 * Privacy is left (computed as WEP). Acting on it would remove
+	 * the network and with it the connected service.
+	 *
+	 * Leave the network as it is. bss->security is recomputed on
+	 * every property change, so the next good scan result brings
+	 * it back in line, and a real change is applied by the first
+	 * update after the association has gone.
+	 */
+	if (old_security != bss->security &&
+			network == interface->current_network &&
+			bss == network->best_bss) {
+		SUPPLICANT_DBG("Ignoring security change of connected BSS "
+			       "%s with path %s", bss->ssid, bss->path);
+	} else if (old_security != bss->security) {
 		struct g_supplicant_bss *new_bss;
 
 		SUPPLICANT_DBG("New network security for %s with path %s",
